I wanted to make a film about kind of  the last town and I got interested in  what was at the very very bottom of the  road in Louisiana all the way out in the  marsh and I started driving down to the  end of every road and when I got down to  this town called ile de Jean Charles I  drove down that tiny little road that  you see in the last shot of the film and  you get to this place that's completely  isolated from the world and going  through a lot of the things that are  happening in the film and so I sort of  started with that and then at the same  time was working on Lucy's play and you  can maybe talk a little bit about where  that comes from it was about a child  named hush puppy who because it was so  close to my heart was a little boy I  wrote it about my my relationship with  my dad after he got very sick and was I  was dealing with my dad's mortality for  the first time and it was so personal to  me that I just couldn't make it about  myself so I made I made it a little boy  and we switched it back to a girl we  switched it back to in bed and I in our  first meeting I think he says this is a  little girl  this is Hush Puppies a little girl and  I'm so grateful that he did that  audition was something that you would  never expect for audition my mom's  friend just like called and said they  had an audition at the library that was  for six to nine year olds and I was only  five so I just went to see if I could  get to try but in the middle of that my  mom was like she could not go she's too  young and my mom's ranma's like you sure  about that one so it was just something  that it was just like a try for so we  just went away like we just went and did  something that five-year-old wouldn't do  so we just went and tried and they call  back like two days later and said they  had audition well they had like a  callback and the person who was on the  phone with Michael got walk one of the  producers and he say he was looking for  naazy my mom was a nice  see you must be looking for Chris and me  they were like oh no he was like oh no  we must have caught the wrong person my  mom was like she stopped her and she  said wait I know my my daughter audition  so you must she must did you answer her  name or did you read it off with a sheet  and they were Michael was like we asked  her name that's why she must have told  you nazy but that's her nickname and her  real name is convincing me so he was  like oh yes we're looking for Nazy so it  was something that a five year old  wouldn't like they were just they would  just react to what they say and it was  just something at my young age I would  just answer what a dog says whenever  they called and said I had the part I  was just like a normal kid on a computer  and they just call and I was like yes  and I went right back to the computer  prefer a part we looked at over 4,000  kids over the course of about nine  months and the way that we work is you  know the we don't sort of finish the  script and then cast the film and then  location scout the film we try to do all  those things at the same time so it  gives us an ability to kind of  incorporate the people that were casting  into the roles and sort of it becomes an  almost research project so you know for  Dwight's character we actually met  Dwight because he runs a bakery that was  directly across the street from our  casting office and so we be in there in  the morning you know getting doughnuts  and coffee and hanging out with Dwight  and you know we actually thought that we  were gonna have to cast that role  professionally because of how difficult  it was but he came in on a whim one day  and you know as we sort of work with and  we started to see that we actually had  someone that was gonna be able to rise  to the occasion and actually bring his  life to this character in a way that was  so much more visceral than anything we'd  seen with with any of the actors we were  working with the BP oil spill happened  on day one 